WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.389 --> 00:00:03.109
Hi, how are you doing?

2
00:00:03.109 --> 00:00:06.389
In this lesson, we'll be exploring parent and instance

3
00:00:06.389 --> 00:00:09.429
materials, as well as expanding their features to make

4
00:00:09.429 --> 00:00:11.990
them a bit more dynamic.

5
00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:15.310
All right, let's get started.

6
00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:17.949
So before diving into the creation of these materials,

7
00:00:17.949 --> 00:00:21.250
I just wanted to emphasize that it is a common production

8
00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:24.510
practice to first create the parent materials,

9
00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:28.070
and then their child or instance materials.

10
00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:30.109
And those instance materials will be the ones

11
00:00:30.109 --> 00:00:34.740
applied to the objects, not their parents.

12
00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:38.020
This will allow you to be faster at iterating the materials,

13
00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:40.600
because A, instance materials don't

14
00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:42.659
need to be recompiled whenever you change them,

15
00:00:42.659 --> 00:00:46.179
and B, if you need to make some drastic changes to how

16
00:00:46.179 --> 00:00:49.340
your whole environment was shaded because, I don't know,

17
00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:50.880
your director had a different vision

18
00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:53.740
for the look of the roughness of the materials, for example.

19
00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:57.700
If most of it derives from two or three master materials,

20
00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:00.100
then you'll just need to change those two or three,

21
00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:03.020
and these changes will be applied like a cascade

22
00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:06.750
to all their children or instance materials.

23
00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:09.390
So this is a very optimized and efficient way

24
00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:11.870
of working with materials in Unreal Engine,

25
00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:15.450
and I would highly recommend it, actually.

26
00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:20.230
OK, let's take a look at an example in Engine now.

27
00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:23.469
All right, we are in the Expanding Material Features

28
00:01:23.469 --> 00:01:26.870
section of our map, which should be fired up

29
00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:30.269
whenever you open the project for the first time.

30
00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:33.590
And this is our sample material for the class.

31
00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:36.230
If we click on the Preview Mesh,

32
00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:39.709
you can find downhill in the Details panel the material

33
00:01:39.709 --> 00:01:42.890
that is assigned to it, and if you double-click on this,

34
00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:45.269
it will open up the material.

35
00:01:45.269 --> 00:01:48.590
But it looks a little different than any other material

36
00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:51.670
we're used to because there is no graph, no nodes,

37
00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:54.549
no setup, and that's because this is actually

38
00:01:54.549 --> 00:01:55.890
an instant material.

39
00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:57.769
And these kind of materials are really useful

40
00:01:57.769 --> 00:02:00.930
because they expose different parameters that we can just

41
00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:04.329
turn on and off to enable and disable,

42
00:02:04.329 --> 00:02:06.849
and then we can just change them on the fly.

43
00:02:06.849 --> 00:02:09.889
So we could be changing these textures to a different one

44
00:02:09.889 --> 00:02:11.490
if we wanted to.

45
00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:15.090
We could be enabling the albedo, the normal, or the roughness

46
00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:17.729
map, and we could also be changing the offset

47
00:02:17.729 --> 00:02:20.939
and the tiling of these maps.

48
00:02:20.939 --> 00:02:25.719
Now, down here, we have this metallic question mark,

49
00:02:25.719 --> 00:02:27.800
and this is not Unreal Engine talking to us.

50
00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:30.280
This is actually something that we set up.

51
00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:33.599
And if we turn it on, for example,

52
00:02:33.599 --> 00:02:38.400
you can see that it will enable a metalness map as well

53
00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:40.919
as the metalness intensity parameter

54
00:02:40.919 --> 00:02:43.680
that we can just adjust as we want.

55
00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:46.060
And if we turn metallic off, you

56
00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:49.639
can see that these two different parameters are swapped

57
00:02:49.639 --> 00:02:52.000
for the base metallic value, which

58
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:56.400
acts as a basically full, flat black and white texture that

59
00:02:56.439 --> 00:03:00.560
increases or decreases the metalness of the material.

60
00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:04.280
OK, so this metallic question mark

61
00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:07.240
is actually named a static switch parameter,

62
00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:09.800
and it is pretty useful at optimizing environments,

63
00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:13.080
for example, because you can turn off a set of textures

64
00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:15.330
if they're not needed.

65
00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:17.449
So let's go back to the slides for just a second

66
00:03:17.449 --> 00:03:21.909
before building this material up in the engine.

67
00:03:21.909 --> 00:03:24.310
OK, so each step that I will be taking now

68
00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:26.430
in Unreal during the demo will also

69
00:03:26.469 --> 00:03:28.909
be displayed through screenshots and instructions

70
00:03:28.909 --> 00:03:32.069
here in the slides, so you can easily follow along.

71
00:03:32.069 --> 00:03:33.949
And together with the sample material

72
00:03:33.949 --> 00:03:35.990
that we just saw, the instance one,

73
00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:38.169
as well as its parent material, you

74
00:03:38.169 --> 00:03:40.430
should be able to just walk in the right direction

75
00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:42.590
as we draft these materials out.

76
00:03:42.590 --> 00:03:44.949
OK, so let's just jump back into the engine

77
00:03:44.949 --> 00:03:46.870
and build our static switch parameter,

78
00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:49.270
as well as some few more nodes to make our materials

79
00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:50.469
a bit more dynamic.

80
00:03:50.469 --> 00:03:52.889
Let's go.

81
00:03:52.889 --> 00:03:55.889
OK, I'm going to be keeping this instance material open,

82
00:03:55.889 --> 00:03:59.110
and then I'm going to go back to my viewport.

83
00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:02.550
And then I will be creating a new material in the content

84
00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:04.069
browser.

85
00:04:04.069 --> 00:04:06.710
I'm going to be calling it m underscore demo,

86
00:04:06.710 --> 00:04:08.550
but you can call it whatever you want,

87
00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:10.949
and then double-click to open it up.

88
00:04:10.949 --> 00:04:13.909
OK, so this graph is completely empty,

89
00:04:13.909 --> 00:04:15.710
but we have already gone through building

90
00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:17.430
a simple material from the ground

91
00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:21.569
up in the previous classes, so we won't be going over it

92
00:04:21.569 --> 00:04:23.389
again in this class.

93
00:04:23.389 --> 00:04:24.790
So to make our life easier, we'll

94
00:04:24.829 --> 00:04:28.910
be just stealing some nodes from the sample material instead.

95
00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:30.750
In order to do that, I will just

96
00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:32.870
go back to our instance material,

97
00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:36.870
and down here I can find the parent material where

98
00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:38.790
this child originates from.

99
00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:42.029
So I will just double-click here to open it up.

100
00:04:42.029 --> 00:04:45.009
OK, so this set of nodes of this parent material

101
00:04:45.009 --> 00:04:48.589
is also ultimately what we'll try to build today.

102
00:04:48.589 --> 00:04:51.639
So you can use that to guide you along.

103
00:04:51.639 --> 00:04:55.079
And by the way, over here you have

104
00:04:55.079 --> 00:04:59.519
a bit of a more complex set of nodes to set up the albedo.

105
00:04:59.519 --> 00:05:02.279
So you can look this up if you want to.

106
00:05:02.279 --> 00:05:05.560
Keep in mind that this setup is using two material

107
00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:08.959
functions, which are the blend overly and the shift contrast,

108
00:05:08.959 --> 00:05:11.120
and we'll go over material functions

109
00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:13.319
in the last class of this course.

110
00:05:13.319 --> 00:05:18.480
OK, now let's just pan back and shift-drag the albedo

111
00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:22.040
sets of nodes, and then Control and click the metalness,

112
00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:25.379
and then Control-C to copy these whole sets of nodes

113
00:05:25.379 --> 00:05:26.639
to borrow it.

114
00:05:26.639 --> 00:05:30.250
Then we'll go back to the material demo,

115
00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:35.829
and Control-V here in the empty graph to paste them.

116
00:05:35.829 --> 00:05:39.829
OK, so we won't be applying any further changes

117
00:05:39.829 --> 00:05:42.949
to the albedo setup, so we can just

118
00:05:42.949 --> 00:05:44.829
take the output pin of the lerp

119
00:05:44.829 --> 00:05:47.680
and then connect it to the base color.

120
00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:51.279
And by the way, we'll be looking into the functionality

121
00:05:51.279 --> 00:05:53.399
of the lerp or linear interpolate node

122
00:05:53.399 --> 00:05:56.800
in the next class when we start developing mass

123
00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:57.800
materials.

124
00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:00.319
OK, let's just go on and expand a little bit more

125
00:06:00.319 --> 00:06:04.079
the functionalities of our metalness.

126
00:06:04.079 --> 00:06:05.800
So first of all, what we want to do

127
00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:09.079
is adding a scalar parameter down here

128
00:06:09.079 --> 00:06:14.230
and call it metallic intensity.

129
00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:16.629
All right, so before moving on in the development

130
00:06:16.629 --> 00:06:19.069
of our material, I just wanted to highlight

131
00:06:19.069 --> 00:06:23.069
the difference between scalar parameter and constant values.

132
00:06:23.069 --> 00:06:25.310
Because if I press 1 on the keyboard

133
00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:28.870
and then click on the graph, I will create a constant value.

134
00:06:28.870 --> 00:06:31.269
If I press S and left click on the graph,

135
00:06:31.269 --> 00:06:33.509
I will create a scalar parameter.

136
00:06:33.509 --> 00:06:37.629
And both of them actually output single float values.

137
00:06:37.629 --> 00:06:39.029
But the difference between them

138
00:06:39.029 --> 00:06:41.189
is that the constant value is not

139
00:06:41.189 --> 00:06:45.149
exposed to material instances while the scalar parameter is.

140
00:06:45.149 --> 00:06:47.189
And that's really the only difference.

141
00:06:47.230 --> 00:06:49.509
But you can convert one to the other

142
00:06:49.509 --> 00:06:51.430
easily by right clicking on them

143
00:06:51.430 --> 00:06:54.310
and then converting to constant for the scalar.

144
00:06:54.310 --> 00:06:57.709
Or for the constant value, you can just right click on it

145
00:06:57.709 --> 00:06:59.189
and then convert to parameter.

146
00:06:59.189 --> 00:07:02.879
So they're easily interchangeable.

147
00:07:02.879 --> 00:07:05.879
OK, now let's just move on and add a multiply node

148
00:07:05.879 --> 00:07:09.959
by pressing M on the keyboard and left clicking on the graph.

149
00:07:09.959 --> 00:07:13.720
Then collect the RGB value of the metalness map with the A

150
00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:16.079
and then the metallic intensity to the B.

151
00:07:16.079 --> 00:07:18.079
We'll then create another scalar parameter

152
00:07:18.079 --> 00:07:23.740
by hitting S on the keyboard, and we'll call it base metal.

153
00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:26.500
And this will just be a flat black and white metallic

154
00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:29.300
texture applied to the whole material.

155
00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:31.899
OK, so now we want a way to switch

156
00:07:31.899 --> 00:07:37.939
between the metallic texture and intensity group of nodes

157
00:07:37.939 --> 00:07:41.569
and the base metal scalar parameter.

158
00:07:41.569 --> 00:07:44.329
And in order to do that, we will add,

159
00:07:44.410 --> 00:07:52.339
if I right click on the graph, a static switch parameter.

160
00:07:52.339 --> 00:07:56.699
And we'll call it metallic question mark.

161
00:07:56.699 --> 00:08:00.850
So do we want the metallic texture to be on or off?

162
00:08:00.850 --> 00:08:03.610
So we'll just connect the multiply node to the true

163
00:08:03.610 --> 00:08:07.290
and then the base metal scalar parameter to the false.

164
00:08:07.290 --> 00:08:08.850
So the static switch parameter will

165
00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:11.490
allow us to flip between using the texture

166
00:08:11.529 --> 00:08:15.769
and the intensity or just one base flat black and white

167
00:08:15.769 --> 00:08:19.629
texture to set up the metalness.

168
00:08:19.629 --> 00:08:23.029
So then we can just plug the output pin of the metallic

169
00:08:23.029 --> 00:08:25.990
to the metallic input of the material.

170
00:08:25.990 --> 00:08:29.670
OK, let's just apply these changes, save,

171
00:08:29.670 --> 00:08:32.710
and then we will control spacebar

172
00:08:32.710 --> 00:08:36.870
and create a misnus material of this parent material.

173
00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:38.950
By the way, they're called parent materials,

174
00:08:38.950 --> 00:08:41.909
but they're actually just generic materials.

175
00:08:41.950 --> 00:08:44.190
I call them parents because we will be creating instance

176
00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:46.669
materials out of them, and they will be their children,

177
00:08:46.669 --> 00:08:48.789
so they're parents to me.

178
00:08:48.789 --> 00:08:51.549
All right, so let's right click on the material demo

179
00:08:51.549 --> 00:08:54.379
and then create a material instance.

180
00:08:54.379 --> 00:08:56.980
And the naming convention for material instances

181
00:08:56.980 --> 00:09:00.940
is usually MI for material instance underscore,

182
00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:04.970
whatever you want to call it, in this case, demo.

183
00:09:04.970 --> 00:09:08.169
OK, then I will double click to open it up,

184
00:09:08.169 --> 00:09:11.169
and I will just dock it over here.

185
00:09:11.169 --> 00:09:12.929
So the first thing I want to test out

186
00:09:12.929 --> 00:09:16.009
is whether the metallic static switch parameter is working.

187
00:09:16.009 --> 00:09:18.690
So in order to turn the switch parameter on,

188
00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:20.529
we first need to enable it.

189
00:09:20.529 --> 00:09:23.090
So I will just enable it from here on the left,

190
00:09:23.090 --> 00:09:26.610
and then I can just turn it on from here with the tick

191
00:09:26.610 --> 00:09:27.529
box.

192
00:09:27.529 --> 00:09:32.090
OK, so once it's turned on, it's working as we need it

193
00:09:32.090 --> 00:09:35.649
because it's displaying the metalness texture that we

194
00:09:35.649 --> 00:09:38.289
can just swap on the fly, and it's also enabling

195
00:09:38.289 --> 00:09:40.330
the metallic intensity scaler.

196
00:09:40.370 --> 00:09:42.090
By the way, these two scaler parameters

197
00:09:42.090 --> 00:09:44.289
are not grouped correctly, but that's fine

198
00:09:44.289 --> 00:09:46.169
because we already talked about grouping

199
00:09:46.169 --> 00:09:47.450
in the previous classes.

200
00:09:47.450 --> 00:09:50.129
So we won't be looking into that today.

201
00:09:50.129 --> 00:09:52.690
There's going to be a little bit organized, but that's fine.

202
00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:55.330
OK, then if we turn off metallic,

203
00:09:55.330 --> 00:09:59.090
you can see that the base metal parameter is turned on,

204
00:09:59.090 --> 00:10:03.059
and this will really act as a flat texture.

205
00:10:03.059 --> 00:10:06.539
All right, so let's just keep this material instance open

206
00:10:06.539 --> 00:10:09.059
and go back to our paint material.

207
00:10:09.100 --> 00:10:10.860
Now we'll be creating a set of nodes

208
00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:13.580
to be able to dynamically control the UV tiling

209
00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:15.860
and offset of our texture.

210
00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:18.700
So let's just pan a little bit more to the left,

211
00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:21.779
and then if we right-click in an empty area of the graph,

212
00:10:21.779 --> 00:10:25.580
we can add a texture coordinate node.

213
00:10:25.580 --> 00:10:27.259
And the texture coordinates will let

214
00:10:27.259 --> 00:10:30.379
us control how our texture will map into our model.

215
00:10:30.379 --> 00:10:33.580
After that, we want to add a scaler parameter that we

216
00:10:33.580 --> 00:10:38.110
will call Tiling Overall.

217
00:10:38.110 --> 00:10:40.029
And the tiling of a texture really

218
00:10:40.029 --> 00:10:42.830
defines how it is scaled.

219
00:10:42.830 --> 00:10:45.029
So let's turn it to 1, else it's just

220
00:10:45.029 --> 00:10:47.620
going to be scaled to 0.

221
00:10:47.620 --> 00:10:50.139
OK, so now we want to be multiplying the texture

222
00:10:50.139 --> 00:10:51.539
coordinates with the tiling.

223
00:10:51.539 --> 00:10:54.620
So I'm going to press M, and left-clicking the graph,

224
00:10:54.620 --> 00:10:57.820
and then connect the texture coordinate to the A,

225
00:10:57.820 --> 00:11:01.419
and then the tiling to the B.

226
00:11:01.419 --> 00:11:04.820
So this set of nodes lets us have a very generic kind

227
00:11:04.820 --> 00:11:06.740
of control over our textures, but we

228
00:11:06.740 --> 00:11:08.620
can get a little bit more granular

229
00:11:08.620 --> 00:11:13.100
by adding two more scalar parameters.

230
00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:21.649
And we'll call one Offset U, turn it on to 1,

231
00:11:21.649 --> 00:11:26.850
and then Offset V, and turn it on to 1.

232
00:11:26.850 --> 00:11:30.730
So the Offset U and V will let us control individually

233
00:11:30.730 --> 00:11:34.570
the x and y vectors of our texture,

234
00:11:34.570 --> 00:11:39.409
where the U controls the horizontal axis left to right,

235
00:11:39.409 --> 00:11:43.009
and the V controls the vertical axis bottom to top.

236
00:11:43.009 --> 00:11:46.169
OK, these two scalar parameters, as we just said,

237
00:11:46.169 --> 00:11:49.009
output single float values.

238
00:11:49.009 --> 00:11:53.490
But the texture coordinate nodes work with vector 2 values.

239
00:11:53.490 --> 00:11:56.769
So we need a way to combine these two scalar parameters

240
00:11:56.769 --> 00:11:57.570
together.

241
00:11:57.570 --> 00:11:59.610
And there is a really useful node for that,

242
00:11:59.610 --> 00:12:01.210
which is called Append Node.

243
00:12:01.210 --> 00:12:08.299
So if I drag out of the Offset U and an Append Vector Node,

244
00:12:08.340 --> 00:12:12.820
if I Control-Alt, Undo will tell me what this node does,

245
00:12:12.820 --> 00:12:15.620
and basically allows me to combine channels together

246
00:12:15.620 --> 00:12:18.059
to create a vector with more channels than the original,

247
00:12:18.059 --> 00:12:20.100
which is actually what we need.

248
00:12:20.100 --> 00:12:22.299
OK, so now we have two different options.

249
00:12:22.299 --> 00:12:26.620
We can either multiply these two nodes or add them.

250
00:12:26.620 --> 00:12:29.299
Using the Add Node, we will be shuffling the U and V

251
00:12:29.299 --> 00:12:32.740
values very linearly without any scaling or distortion,

252
00:12:32.740 --> 00:12:34.740
while using the Multiply Node,

253
00:12:34.740 --> 00:12:37.259
they will actually scale a little bit.

254
00:12:37.259 --> 00:12:39.899
And I actually think that the Multiply Node usually

255
00:12:39.899 --> 00:12:42.580
looks a little bit more natural, so I will just

256
00:12:42.580 --> 00:12:44.450
go with that for now.

257
00:12:44.450 --> 00:12:47.169
So let's connect the Multiply Node to the A

258
00:12:47.169 --> 00:12:50.529
and the Append Node to the B. And I will keep the Add Node

259
00:12:50.529 --> 00:12:52.649
here for now, because I want to show you the difference

260
00:12:52.649 --> 00:12:54.370
between using the two.

261
00:12:54.370 --> 00:12:56.970
So then we want to connect the Multiply Output

262
00:12:56.970 --> 00:13:01.289
Pane to the UVs of the Albedo Map and the Metallus.

263
00:13:01.289 --> 00:13:04.960
OK, let's just test this out by applying.

264
00:13:04.960 --> 00:13:07.799
And then I will just undock the Material Instance,

265
00:13:07.799 --> 00:13:10.159
because I want to show the difference between multiplying

266
00:13:10.159 --> 00:13:10.840
and applying.

267
00:13:10.840 --> 00:13:16.450
So I will expand this a little bit,

268
00:13:16.450 --> 00:13:21.700
and I will set the Preview Mode to Plane up here,

269
00:13:21.700 --> 00:13:23.779
just so that this is a little bit clearer.

270
00:13:23.779 --> 00:13:26.940
And then I will enable the Offset U, Offset V,

271
00:13:26.940 --> 00:13:28.580
and the Tiling Overall.

272
00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:30.820
So the Tiling Overall, as we just said,

273
00:13:30.820 --> 00:13:34.879
is just a very generic scaling of the Texture.

274
00:13:34.879 --> 00:13:37.159
And then the Offset U will just

275
00:13:37.159 --> 00:13:39.399
stretch it in the horizontal axis

276
00:13:39.399 --> 00:13:42.360
and the V in the vertical axis.

277
00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:46.399
OK, so this is how multiplying them looks like.

278
00:13:46.399 --> 00:13:49.279
Now let's switch back to adding them instead

279
00:13:49.279 --> 00:13:51.730
in the Material demo.

280
00:13:51.730 --> 00:13:54.649
And if I Control and hold left-click,

281
00:13:54.649 --> 00:13:58.590
I can just drag this Input Pin.

282
00:13:58.590 --> 00:14:01.549
And I can do the same with the B

283
00:14:01.549 --> 00:14:05.399
and with the output of the Multiply.

284
00:14:05.399 --> 00:14:08.480
So remember that we need to apply these changes.

285
00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:11.279
And now, as you can see, there is no more stretching

286
00:14:11.279 --> 00:14:16.039
on the Texture whenever we Offset U and Offset V.

287
00:14:16.039 --> 00:14:18.129
So it's way more linear.

288
00:14:18.129 --> 00:14:19.929
All right, so that was it for this class.

289
00:14:19.929 --> 00:14:20.929
I hope you enjoyed it.

290
00:14:20.929 --> 00:14:22.370
And in the next one, we will be

291
00:14:22.370 --> 00:14:24.210
learning about Mask Materials.

292
00:14:24.210 --> 00:14:27.409
So stick around, because it's going to be pretty cool.
